export const DE_C2_S05_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-die-waescherin-des-geheimrats": [
    `Weimar, 14 October 1823. Your Excellency, most honourable Privy Councillor! Forgive a person of my station for daring to come before you with a pen instead of a laundry basket. I am the widow Dorothea Kühn of the Jakobsvorstadt, and for eleven years I have done the washing of your house on the Frauenplan: the shirts, the cravats and, I mention it only with a blush, the rest as well. You will not know me; one knows the washerwoman as little as the water she washes in, and that is as it should be. Nor would I be writing to you today had an incident, in which I am not entirely innocent, not robbed me of my sleep.`,
    `It is Your Excellency's habit (your servant Stadelmann confirmed it to me under the seal of secrecy) to write on the cuffs of your shirts at night, when something occurs to you and no paper is at hand. I have washed those cuffs for eleven years. With respect, I have had more of your ideas in my lye than many a professor in Jena has had on his lectern. Mostly they were numbers, names of stones, once a bill for peat that did not add up. I washed them out the way one washes out a red-wine stain, without giving it a thought.`,
    `Last Tuesday, however, on the left sleeve of the fine cambric shirt which, so they say in town, you wore on your return from Marienbad, I found eight lines of verse. They were written in pencil, very small, and crossed out in two places. I confess that I read them. I confess further that I read them twice and then a third time, while the water in the copper went cold. They spoke of a farewell, of a carriage driving away, and of a heart which, so it said, was older than the man who carries it.`,
    `Your Excellency will object that a washerwoman is not to read verses but to wash them. That is true, and I washed them. I made up the lye as is proper and rubbed the cuff, and the pencil went the way pencil goes, without resistance and without complaint. Only when the shirt was hanging on the line did it occur to me that there might be no second copy of those lines. I did not sleep a wink that night. As a widow one is used to losses; but one does not like to be someone else's loss oneself.`,
    `So on Wednesday I sat down and wrote the verses out from memory, on the paper my late husband used for his promissory notes. I enclose them with this letter. I beg you to consider that I only attended the village school in Oberweimar and that my memory is good but not perfect. In two places I was not sure of myself. Where, if I remember rightly, Your Excellency had written "pain", I now have "comfort", because it rhymed just as well and because I felt that a man of seventy-four deserves a little comfort. The last line, however, the one that was crossed out, I did not leave out but wrote out in full, for I believe it was the best.`,
    `I know very well that in doing so I take a liberty that is not mine to take. A professor would call it a falsification. I call it a mending, such as I have been doing on your stockings for eleven years without your ever noticing. You have never complained about the darned places in your stockings either, and some of them, I venture to say, hold better than the original.`,
    `Should Your Excellency reject my copy, simply throw it in the fire; I shall never know and shall take no offence. But should you, as I hardly dare hope, be able to use the lines after all, I permit myself one single request: in future, write on paper. Pencil is hard to get out of cambric, and I am no longer young enough to rub twice, and not yet old enough to stay indifferent when something beautiful disappears in the water.`,
    `With most humble respect I remain Your Excellency's most obedient servant, Dorothea Kühn, washerwoman. Postscript: your servant Stadelmann asks me to let you know that the shirt is finished and ready. This time I laid it in lavender. They say in town that you left behind a young lady in Marienbad who was fond of lavender. I know nothing about it. I only wash.`,
  ],
  "de-der-solidaritaetsbeitrag": [
    `The form had eleven pages, and Leonie had printed it out, hole-punched it and put it in a folder as if it were a seminar paper. She put it on the living-room table next to the meringues her grandmother had bought because a visitor was coming from Bern, and explained what it was all about. The federal government, she said, had passed a law. Anyone who as a child had been farmed out, put in a home or otherwise placed with strangers by the authorities was entitled to a solidarity contribution, twenty-five thousand francs, tax-free. All you had to do was fill in the application and describe what had happened.`,
    `Rosmarie Zaugg was eighty-one and had not described what had happened for seventy-six years, not to her husband, who had guessed and never asked, and not to her son, Leonie's father, who had only learned of it from the newspaper when the whole affair suddenly appeared everywhere a few years ago, as if a cellar had been discovered in Switzerland that nobody claimed to have known about. She had been five when her mother died and the local council of Trubschachen decided that her father, who drank, could not bring up four children. The children were distributed like seed. Rosmarie went to a farm above Eggiwil, to a farmer called Gerber, for a boarding allowance which the municipality paid and which was roughly what one spent on a calf.`,
    `Leonie read out the questions. Surname and first name. Date of birth. Period of the measure. Please briefly describe the harm suffered. Rosmarie found the word "harm" (literally "impairments") remarkable. It sounded like water damage. She thought of the room above the cowshed, where in winter it was so cold that the water froze in the jug, of getting up at half past four, of the hour-long walk to school that she made after milking, and of the teacher who asked her in front of the whole class why she smelled of cow. She thought of Gerber, who did not beat her, at least not often, and of his son, about whom she did not think, never, not even now. "Impairments," she said. "What am I supposed to write there?"`,
    `"Everything," said Leonie. "The more precise, the better. This isn't a court, Grandma. Nobody has to prove anything." Rosmarie took the ballpoint pen, wrote her name in block capitals, her date of birth, the period, 1941 to 1951, and then paused. Outside in the pasture stood the neighbour's cattle, and a cow was rubbing itself against a fence post with the thoroughness of a creature that knows no forms. Then Rosmarie wrote five lines in the big empty box, slowly and without stopping, turned the sheet over and pushed it across to Leonie.`,
    `Leonie read: "Every Sunday after church Frau Gerber put a piece of plaited bread in my apron without the farmer seeing. She never said anything. In the winter of 1944 she gave me her own socks and wore none herself. She died in 1979. I want this to be written down somewhere." Leonie read it twice. Then she said carefully, the way one speaks to a child who has misunderstood the task, that this was lovely, but the office wanted to know something else. The injustice, Grandma. Not what was good.`,
    `"The office knows about the injustice," said Rosmarie. "It's in all the newspapers. There are books about it and an exhibition that has travelled all over the country, and a federal councillor has apologised in the name of the country. The injustice is official now. But that Frau Gerber went without socks, nobody knows that except me. And when I am dead, nobody will know it any more." She took a meringue, broke it in two and put one half on Leonie's plate. "Twenty-five thousand francs," she said. "For what happened. Do you know what a calf costs today?"`,
    `Leonie sent off the application in February, with the five lines and with a second page that she wrote herself, after a long phone call with her father and after studying files she had ordered from the State Archives in Bern, on which it said in neat official handwriting that the Zaugg girl was "healthy, strong and suitable for field work". In the autumn the decision came. The application had been approved. A week later the twenty-five thousand francs were in the account.`,
    `Rosmarie withdrew two thousand of it and had Leonie drive her to Eggiwil. In the cemetery they searched for half an hour before they found the grave, weathered, half overgrown with ivy, a stone with two names: Gerber Ernst and Gerber-Wüthrich Anna. Rosmarie did not have the stonemason in Signau carve anything new. She only had the second name freshly gone over, in gold leaf, and not the first. The stonemason asked whether she really wanted it like that; it would look uneven. "It was uneven," said Rosmarie.`,
  ],
  "de-das-weisswurst-urteil": [
    `Freising District Court, case number 3 C 1212/25. In the name of the people, in the legal dispute between Dr Hartmut Wendeborn, resident in Bielefeld, claimant, and Mr Xaver Obermaier, innkeeper, proprietor of the inn "Zum Grünen Baum" in Freising, defendant, concerning damages and a declaratory judgment, the following judgment is given: the claim is dismissed. The claimant bears the costs of the proceedings. The judgment is provisionally enforceable.`,
    `Facts of the case. The claimant, a retired administrator with a doctorate, entered the defendant's inn on Saturday, 12 April 2025, at 12.04 p.m. (the time is undisputed between the parties) and ordered from the waitress, Ms Resi Gschwendtner, two white sausages together with sweet mustard and a pretzel. Ms Gschwendtner refused to take the order, remarking that the midday bells had already rung. When the claimant asked on what legal basis this refusal rested, she declared that everybody knew that, and added a remark whose exact wording is disputed between the parties, but in which the court was able to make out beyond doubt the word "Preiß" (Prussian). The claimant thereupon left the inn without having eaten and, by his own account "deeply hurt", took the bus back to his hotel in Moosburg.`,
    `The claimant takes the view that a guest is entitled to be served as long as a dish is listed on the menu without any time restriction, irrespective of the striking of the church bell. The so-called twelve o'clock rule, he argues, is neither laid down by law nor noted on the menu and therefore constitutes a surprise clause. He claims reimbursement of travel costs incurred of 14.80 euros and a declaration that the white sausage is a foodstuff that may be eaten at any time of day. The defendant asks for the claim to be dismissed. He submits that the white sausage must not hear the noon bell; this has always been so and needs no justification, least of all to someone from Bielefeld.`,
    `Reasons for the decision. The claim is admissible but unfounded. It is true that the twelve o'clock rule is not reflected in any law, any regulation or, as far as can be seen, any by-law of the town of Freising. Nor does the court fail to recognise that its derivation from the poor keeping quality of the sausage meat in the days before refrigeration is today of merely historical significance. Nevertheless, it is a custom generally known within the area of the so-called white-sausage equator, a common practice within the meaning of section 157 of the Civil Code, knowledge of which can reasonably be expected of an average, sensible guest.`,
    `The claimant's objection that he comes from East Westphalia and could not have known the custom does not hold. Whoever enters the area where a custom applies must familiarise himself with it, just as it is incumbent on a traveller to England to find out which side of the road to drive on. In addition, by his own submission the claimant has visited the Freising area every year since 1987, and according to the credible testimony of the witness Gschwendtner he frequents the inn in question "more often than the parish priest". The court is therefore convinced that the claimant knew the rule. It has rather gained the impression that, for the claimant, it was less about the sausage than about the principle.`,
    `The application for a declaratory judgment also fails. Whether the white sausage is a foodstuff that may be eaten all day is not a legal question but a question of world view, on which a secular court must refrain from ruling. The application would in any case be too vague, since it leaves open whether the all-day eating sought is also meant to cover the hours of the night. The court further points out that at 12.04 p.m. the claimant was offered roast pork with dumplings, which, as the witness credibly testified, he turned down "with a face like at a tax audit". An innkeeper is under no obligation to protect his guests from the consequences of their own obstinacy.`,
    `The decision on costs is based on section 91 of the Code of Civil Procedure. The court further notes that after the close of the oral hearing, which ended at 11.20 a.m., the parties left the courtroom together and, as the court has been informed by a third party, were sitting at one table in the "Grüner Baum" at 11.35 a.m., where the defendant served the claimant two white sausages on the house. The claimant is said to have eaten them not with knife and fork but by sucking them out of their skins in the local manner. The court considers legal peace to have been restored. It reserves the right to schedule future hearings in white-sausage matters for the morning only. Signed: Dr Wimmer, judge at the district court.`,
  ],
  "de-daktylus-ueber-tempelhof": [
    `I am ninety-one years old, and the only things that have stayed with me from Latin lessons are a verse that isn't in any book and the knowledge of what a caesura is. I owe both to Dr Ewald Kranich, senior teacher of classical languages at the grammar school in Boelckestraße, and, strictly speaking, I owe both to the Americans.`,
    `In the autumn of 1948 our school, or what was left of it, stood less than two kilometres from Tempelhof airport. The Russians had blocked the roads to Berlin, and since the summer the Western powers had been flying in everything the city needed to live: flour, coal, powdered milk, medicine, supposedly even a power station in pieces. The planes came in a rhythm you soon counted along with in your sleep, one every few minutes, day and night, and when they thundered over our roof, the cardboard windows that no longer had any glass rattled, and no human word could compete.`,
    `I was thirteen, hungry and, as my mother said, already too clever for my age. My real education I received in the afternoons at Zoo station, where I traded American cigarettes for anything that could be traded. That a man like Kranich, seventy, gaunt, with a collar older than I was, wanted to teach us Virgil in the mornings while bread was falling from the sky above us seemed to me like a joke only grown-ups find funny. Arma virumque cano, "Of arms and the man I sing." I preferred to sing of Lucky Strike.`,
    `At first Kranich had tried to talk over the planes. He gave up after three days. Instead he began to teach into the noise. When he heard a plane coming, he raised his hand, the class fell silent, and at his signal we counted the engines along with him: long, short, short. Long, short, short. That, he explained in the silence afterwards, was a dactyl, the finger, one long joint and two short ones, and six of them in a row made a hexameter, the verse of Homer and Virgil. The Americans, he said drily, were therefore the first epic poets of air traffic.`,
    `I wasn't impressed. One morning in November he caught me counting cigarettes under my desk. He didn't confiscate them, as I had expected, but put them back on my desk and said I would get a punishment: a Latin hexameter about the planes, by Monday. I cobbled something together that roughly said the birds of iron were bringing flour, and that didn't scan in a single foot. Kranich read it, nodded as if I had handed him a poem by Horace, and crossed out everything except two words. From them he built, in front of the whole class, on the blackboard, in time with the next plane, the line: "Panem portantes pennae volitant super urbem." Carrying bread, the wings fly over the city.`,
    `After the lesson, when the others had already gone out, I asked him why he never looked up when the planes came. Everyone else looked up; the children waved, the old people sometimes cried. He wiped the blackboard, very thoroughly, and then said, without turning round, that his son had been an air force auxiliary at an anti-aircraft battery in Lichterfelde in 1944, sixteen years old. Back then the engines in the sky had sounded much the same. In that time he had learned not to look up, and he hadn't been able to break the habit. Then he gave me back my punishment. Under the verse it said: "Good. The sky will do the rest."`,
    `The blockade ended on 12 May 1949. The planes kept coming for a while, then less often, and by the autumn the sky over Boelckestraße was as quiet again as skies are really meant to be. In the first Latin lesson in which no more planes came, something strange happened. Kranich was reading a line from the Aeneid aloud, and in the middle of the line, at the point where the noise would once have started, he raised his hand out of habit and stopped. And thirty boys, none of us could have said why, held their breath with him and waited for something that didn't come.`,
    `I don't think it ever again took so long in a classroom for someone to say something. Later I read that the caesura in a hexameter is the break in the middle of the line where the speaker draws breath, a pause that belongs to the verse even though nothing sounds in it. I have experienced many pauses in my life. None was as loud as that one.`,
  ],
  "de-der-abonnent": [
    `Forty-seven years, he said to the young woman standing next to him at the bar in the interval foyer, holding a glass of tap water as if she were holding on to it, for forty-seven years I have sat in the third row, seat fourteen, on a season ticket, and in those forty-seven years I have seen everything, Lear three times, Faust four times, Tasso, which nobody wants to see, six times, I have seen actors die, on stage and afterwards, and never, not a single time, have I left a performance at the interval, but today, he said, today I am close to it, today I am closer to it than ever.`,
    `You have seen, he said, what this person, I don't even know who directed it, I no longer read the programmes, out of self-protection I no longer read them, what this person has made of the play. Minna von Barnhelm in a multi-storey car park! Tellheim with a mobile phone! The young lady in trainers, and the innkeeper, who in Lessing is a perfectly ordinary, shabby, garrulous innkeeper, as a hologram, madam, as a hologram. I have nothing against the present, I live in it myself, of necessity, but I demand that it stays outside when I am sitting in the theatre, that is what I pay for, that is what I have paid for for forty-seven years.`,
    `The young woman said that the car park was perhaps an image, an image of a society in which everyone only parks briefly and drives straight off again. An image, he said, an image, they all say that, young people always say it is an image when nothing has occurred to them. A car park is a car park, madam. For forty-two years I managed a car park, the car park on Schwedenplatz, six levels, four hundred and twelve spaces, I know what I am talking about. In a car park no love takes place, in a car park parking takes place and at weekends vomiting, and the only thing in a car park that is an image is the barrier, which rises when you have paid and does not rise when you have not paid, that is the whole of life, there is no more image than that.`,
    `And then they whisper, he said, they all whisper into their little microphones, which they stick to their cheeks like a plaster. In the old days you understood every word in this house, right up to the gallery; today you understand nothing in the third row, and if you do understand something, you wish you hadn't. The young woman asked why he still came, then. He looked at her as if she had asked why he still breathed. Because I am a subscriber, he said. One does not cancel a subscription just because one no longer likes it, one doesn't cancel a marriage either. My wife did not cancel me for forty-three years, although she did not like me in at least forty of them. She sat next to me, seat fifteen. Seat fifteen has been empty for four years, and I renew it anyway, every year, both seats, that is not sentimentality, madam, that is order.`,
    `The only thing, he then said, lowering his voice as if betraying a state secret, the only thing that is not a complete failure in this whole evening is the neon tube. In the second act, on level minus two, where Tellheim rejects Minna, a neon tube flickers above the two of them, and it flickers exactly the way a neon tube flickers when the ballast has gone, not the way a set designer believes it flickers, but exactly like that. I had a tube like that at Schwedenplatz, level minus two, for nineteen years, I never had it repaired, out of thrift, I said, out of sloppiness, my wife said, and then once she said that this tube was the only poetic thing in my life, and I did not contradict her, because she was right, she was usually right, it was unbearable.`,
    `The young woman said nothing. She turned the water glass in her hand. Then the bell rang for the second time, and a man with headphones round his neck pushed his way through the foyer and stopped in front of her. "Ms Kerschbaumer," he said, out of breath, "the technical crew are asking whether the tube in the second act really should stay like that. The lighting man says it looks broken." The young woman looked at the old man, just for a moment. "It stays like that," she said. "Exactly like that."`,
    `He went back to his seat, third row, seat fourteen, and as always laid his coat on seat fifteen. He did not clap at the end; he had never clapped, he considered clapping a bad habit that only spoils the actors. But when the director came on stage to take her bow and the audience began to boo, as has always been the custom in this house when someone has dared something, he stood up, took his coat from the neighbouring seat and remained standing until she had seen him. The next morning he went to the box office and renewed the season ticket, both seats, for three more years. Order, he said to the woman at the till, must be kept.`,
  ],
};

